    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "the thing is, it feels way more specific than just \"plants\". like, the misting goes off right when i'm sitting on my porch and the smell is so chemical/heavy it's almost like a sedative. i'm worried there might be actual growth stimulants or something in the water being aerosolized. are there specific types of high-grade air filters or maybe even charcoal-based systems that can specifically target chemical aerosols rather than just dust/pollen?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Indeed, chemical aerosols pose a different challenge from just fine particulates or common plant scents. High-grade air filtration systems including those with activated carbon filters can indeed help in reducing odors and potentially some chemical aerosols. Activated carbon works by adsorbing volatile organic compounds (VOCs) and other molecules, which can include certain scents and airborne compounds produced in hydroponic systems. Pleated or replacement activated carbon filters can be more efficient at capturing these volatile compounds compared to basic HEPA filters, which are more effective at capturing larger particles.\n\nAnother solution could be to incorporate an air purifier with an activated carbon filter for your porch area specifically. These can offer substantial protection against chemicals, especially if they are placed closer to the source of the mist, although it's clear that distance is not sufficient in your case.\n\nAdditionally, considering an inline air purifier, which targets an area of air flow, might act as a barrier to the aerosolized chemicals as they are expelled from the system. This can intercept particles before entering your personal space.\n\nIf the hydroponic setup is the cause and you believe there is a direct impact on your well-being, it might be appropriate to request or request from your neighbor the use of impermeable coverings when using the misting system",
      "source": "target",
